SM(612,{"n":"Revelation 3.1-7","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Revelation 3.1-7.mp3","d":2507.2,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[19.92,"Go ahead and pick up in the Book of Revelation, chapter 3, verse 1."],[26.96,"And to the angel of the church in Sardis, these things says he who has the seven spirits of God and the seven stars."],[38.61,"I know your works, that you have a name, that you are alive, but you are dead."],[45.37,"So at the beginning there, as we've seen, there was this description in chapter one where St."],[53.85,"John encountered right the risen Christ in glory."],[57.21,"There were these various descriptors of him, and we've seen how pieces of those descriptors get picked up at the beginning of each of these letters by way of his identification."],[67.21,"So in this case, that's the he who has the seven spirits of God and the seven stars."],[77.37,"Even more negative than the other ones that we heard before."],[82.17,"Several of the ones we read last time sort of started with, well, here's all the good things, but or yet."],[88.91,"And then there would be there's this, there's this other bad thing."],[92.28,"This one just starts out right off the bat."],[94.84,"I know your works."],[96.04,"You have a name that you are alive."],[98.22,"There's the church in Sardis."],[102.16,"But you're dead."],[104.65,"Now obviously he doesn't mean all of the individuals there are dead."],[109.15,"He's talking about, again, the organism of the community."],[112.67,"There's no life in the community."],[121.07,"Verse 2 be watchful and strengthen the things which remain that are ready to die, for I have not found your works perfect before God."],[131.95,"Okay. So he says that they're dead."],[136.99,"He says, now."],[139.43,"Uh be watchful, sort of wake up."],[142.07,"Strengthen the things that are remained that are ready to die."],[144.95,"So apparently there are a few things there, there are a few elements of their life as a community that are not completely dead yet."],[155.92,"There's a Monty Python reference I want to make, but I'll skip it."],[161.68,"So they're almost dead."],[163.36,"They're dying. They're about dead."],[165.96,"But so Christ is saying, those few things that still have some life in them, you need to take hold of those and strengthen them."],[173.08,"Try to resuscitate them."],[174.52,"And maybe through them you can resuscitate, the whole community."],[185.54,"Remember, therefore, how you have received and heard."],[190.26,"Hold fast and repent."],[192.5,"Therefore, if you will not watch, I will come upon you as a thief, and you will not know what hour I will come upon you."],[200.98,"So received and heard."],[205.95,"That's back when they were established."],[208.13,"So he's saying that this isn't that you don't know any better."],[212.15,"You do know better."],[213.91,"And so you need to go back to what you know and you need to repent."],[218.49,"And this isn't a separate command."],[222.67,"So remember, as we've said a lot of times, repentance is not just like feeling guilty and feeling bad or saying, I'm sorry. or doing something to get off the hook for what you've done"],[236.23,"It's actually trying to repair what's broken, trying to heal what's wounded."],[242.79,"And so saying to them repent and saying to them strengthen the things that are still alive."],[247.83,"This is the same thing."],[250.15,"This is what repentance means."],[255.19,"Or I'm going to come, you know."],[257.83,"It's it's you need to do that, you need to turn back to those things, you need to strengthen them, you need to re-establish yourselves, you know, the way the way you were in the beginning."],[267.11,"Otherwise, and this is imagery that's used that Christ uses several times in the Gospels."],[272.48,"When he's talking about his cominging. he says he's going to come like a thief in the night, mean, uh, and he explicitly says, I believe it's in St"],[281.36,"Matthew's gospel. If the owner of the house knew when the thief was coming, the thief wouldn't get very far."],[286.92,"Because the owner would be prepared and would stop the thief."],[290.04,"But the point is, the thief shows up when everyone's asleep, when nobody's watching."],[295.3,"And then does whatever he wants."],[297.48,"And so this is again, we talked before about that language of visiting."],[304.93,"How for us it's like, oh, you know, the bishop's gonna come visit."],[308.21,"That's good. Um thankfully it's good when the bishop comes to visit us here."],[313.87,"Um, but uh this is more like wait till your dad gets home kind of visit."],[317.79,"you' This is know, uh hes coming and he's gonna sort sort some things out."],[324.83,"And so Christ is not only saying, I'm gonna come and sort some things out, but if you don't get on top of this, if you don't begin to repent, if you don't begin to watch and to change, then I'm gonna show up to sort some things out, and you're not gonna see me coming."],[341.17,"And you're gonna get caught, and that's not gonna be good for you."],[349.85,"Verse 4, you have a few names even in Sardis who have not defiled their garments, and they shall walk with me in white, for they are worthy."],[359.21,"Okay, so again, this is what I mean when I say make this distinction between talking to the church as a community, as this communal organism, and talking to the collection of individuals."],[373.19,"Because he says right here, there are certain people, who are going to those Eucharistic gatherings, who are part of that community, who are not dead."],[383.63,"Who are followers of Christ."],[386.19,"And so Christ is making this statement."],[387.79,"When he shows up to sort things out."],[390.21,"It's not like bad things are going to happen to them through guilt by association."],[395.13,"But it's gonna it's gonna be about those who are not, watchful."],[399.15,"So there's the church as this communal organism, and then there are the people who make it up."],[402.67,"Yes, sir When when you first said dead, I pictured a church where when we say a church is dead, nothing's happening there, are anybody's there, nothing's going on."],[416.87,"This sounds more like they are actively doing stuff that is."],[421.51,"Yes. Yes. Yeah, so you're right."],[427.03,"Uh part of that is because we're sort of modern capitalists."],[432.09,"So we judge whether a church is thriving by, oh, they have a budget and giving is high, and there's lots of people in the seats on a Sunday morning, and that's that's a live, vibrant, growing, successful church, and then you know, another church that has fewer people and less money and everything, that's a dying that's a dying church because we have a mixed up standard."],[457.01,"So of course in the first century A D, none of these churches had a budget at all."],[463.23,"And none of them had and it wasn't about trying to pack in all the people you could."],[467.09,"Trying to line up, you know, oh, we had 73 baptisms, you know, this week, and right, trying to recruit everybody in that sense."],[476.7,"In fact, it was difficult to enter one of these Christian communities because a lot of them were under persecution."],[483.26,"So you didn't just accept anybody who walked in off the street."],[486.6,"That's where that's where it comes from that we have like sponsors when someone joins the church."],[490.6,"ch. You had to have someone who was already a Christian at that time who could vou for you"],[497.58,"Like, yeah, this isn't a robot spy, this isn't somebody who's coming in to do XYZ."],[503.08,"Yeah, yeah. And we still have sponsors now."],[506.44,"But now it's it's a little more ceremonial, than it was then."],[510.87,"Um but then it was actually a person vouching for you."],[515.08,"Um so so yeah, they weren't trying to do that."],[519.48,"And so this is about uh spiritual life and s and spiritual death."],[525.26,"So like St."],[525.82,"John of Damascus um said that physical death, is when the soul is separated from the body."],[532.08,"the The life of the body leaves body."],[537.26,"And spiritual death is when the soul is separated from God."],[543.42,"Meaning when our life is separated from the source of life."],[550.35,"And we tend to think of, well, physical death, that's what death really is."],[557.07,"And spiritual death, that's sort of an analogy."],[560.53,"That's kind of a woo-woo spiritual analogy."],[564.77,"But for the perspective of like St."],[566.77,"John, it's the exact opposite."],[570.07,"Real life is being connected to God in Christ."],[573.77,"Who's the source of life"],[576.69,"And uh biological life is sort of an image of that."],[581.29,"And then uh uh so real death is being cut off from God, and physical death for Saint John is not only the image of that, but is the result of that."],[594.59,"So Adam and Eve, when they're expelled from paradise, are spiritually dead, and that they're cut off from the tree of life, they're cut off from the source of life, and that because of that they physically die."],[611.55,"Yes, yes, and so that's right, so this may have been from the perspective of population."],[621.21,"Membership roles or whatever, a thriving, physically alive Christian community."],[628.34,"Or church. But spiritually it's dead."],[633.16,"And that would be like most of Israel's history, for example."],[637.94,"Regardless of whether they were prospering or failing economically and politically, and in terms of expanding their territory in war, they were spiritually dead most of the time."],[648.04,"They were cut off from God."],[650.78,"And eventually, if we're cut off from the source of life long enough, even that physical stuff all collapses and falls apart."],[660.74,"Which is what will happen to the church at Sardis if they don't now repent and turn back."],[670.66,"Verse 5 He who overcomes shall be clothed in white garments."],[674.66,"And I will not blot out his name from the book of life."],[679.46,"But I will confess his name before my father and before his angels."],[683.7,"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches."],[686.9,"So verse 6 there, we've seen that's like at the end of every single letter."],[691.59,"And verse five, before that follows the format we've seen at the end of each letter."],[697.45,"Talking about the reward that will come to he who overcomes."],[700.91,"He who conquers, he who is victorious over this, including those people who will be dressed in white."],[703.95,"And he he makes that explicit"],[709.07,"Those people who are living here in this spiritually dead community, but who are still following Christ, they'll be clothed with white garments."],[717.8,"He says, and all those who overcome will be clothed with white garments."],[723.8,"They will not, their name will not be blotted out from the book of life."],[727.46,"So there's this in this letter, there's this these repeated references to a name."],[735.48,"So he says, You have a name, but you're dead."],[739.58,"What does that mean?"],[740.54,"That that's talking about the name in the book of life."],[742.62,"And we'll get to what the book of life is in a second."],[744.86,"So there's a name in the book of life for that church."],[748.36,"But the church is dead, meaning it's getting close to getting blotted out."],[754.12,"And then he says in verse 4, you have a few names even in Sardis."],[760.21,"Referring to people."],[762.07,"So their names are still there in the book of life, and they're still alive."],[767.17,"Is this the same name that white favorite ball is a lot of white cells?"],[772.61,"No, this is specifically a reference to the book of life."],[775.45,"I'll develop that now, actually."],[778.57,"Um so the book of life is there are literally scattered references to it all through the Bible."],[788.13,"Because the first one's in the Torah, and the last ones are here in Revelation."],[788.77,"'s So it literally scattered all the way through."],[796.33,"And this is an instance of a very widespread ancient Near Eastern tradition that pretty much everyone has."],[806.89,"We're not sure the Sumerians had it, although there's some stuff that kind of looks like it."],[811.05,"But there's lots of tablets that haven't been translated."],[814.17,"If you want to go get a degree in Sumeriology, you can sit around and translate mostly grocery lists for the rest of your life and make a moderate lower middle class living."],[824.09,"But that's just one avenue open to you."],[826.45,"Um yeah, not a lot of people want to do it."],[830.33,"That's why there's so many openings."],[832.13,"Uh but um but the Babylonians have it."],[833.69,"uh The the there's sort of a version of it with the there's an Indo-European version of it that the Greeks kind of have."],[844.53,"Um and uh the uh Egyptians have it, the the um Assyrians had it, etc."],[854.61,"etc. The Syrians, the Hittites."],[857.39,"And it usually takes the form of heavenly books or heavenly tablets."],[862.03,"Usually tablets if you go earlier."],[864.13,"That whatever the the writing method is at the time, they have those in some heavenly place, and there's some kind of divine being, or if we're or angels, or right, some kind of beings."],[877.33,"Um when you when you get to the Indo-European version it's usually fates."],[883.06,"There are these divine beings called fates who are in charge of this kind of thing."],[887.22,"Um the the work, everything a person does, is sort of recorded."],[893.64,"Everything that happens on earth is sort of recorded."],[897.56,"Um and this is imagery to indicate that depending on what culture we're talking about, the divine is aware of everything."],[908.7,"That's going on."],[910.62,"And that uh that people do."],[912.7,"Yes, Santa would be the latest."],[915.9,"Um button. Yeah, yeah, that would be a heavenly database, I guess. if we were doing it today"],[923.94,"Um, but so so all this if information is is uh taken down."],[930.66,"And in particular, we've talked before about the idea of a person's memory."],[937.9,"So when we're talking about like someone dies, in the ancient Near East, they go to Sheol, or Hades, if you're Greek."],[947.16,"Um and that person sort of still exists, at least in some kind of shadowy form, as long as they're remembered."],[957.66,"Then they still have, and then as the memory fades, they sort of fade off."],[963.71,"Um so this is where us singing memory eternal comes from."],[971.09,"This is with this this same idea."],[973.58,"Because the idea is if God remembers you, then that's then that's eternal."],[980.66,"So the idea is if who you are and your work, you notice how at the beginning of each of these letters, or somewhere early on, in every one of them, pretty much, Christ says, I know your works."],[994.6,"He's got the book."],[996.18,"I know your works."],[997.62,"I know what you're doing."],[1000.15,"And so if someone's name is blotted out of there. that means they're forgotten"],[1012.12,"And uh so, for example, in Psalm 88 or 87 that we read at Battens."],[1017.98,"Like the slain that lie in the grave whom thou rememberest no more."],[1022.54,"They are cut off from thy hand."],[1025.2,"Talking to God."],[1026.16,"So that's the imagery of that."],[1029.38,"Of like you're gone if you're blotted out."],[1031.3,"But if you're not blotted out, if God knows your name, the these late, like these names who are clothed in white, the names of the churches, then that's that's an image of of eternal life."],[1044.56,"Because God knows you eternally."],[1045.44,"you eternally. And know God"],[1049.4,"So that's where this book of life and the name symbolism is coming from."],[1054.62,"And where the idea of the name being blotted out, is sort of forgotten, and so you die and you're gone."],[1061.4,"And that's that's how that serves as a warning at the beginning of this letter."],[1066.04,"You still have a name, but you're dead."],[1071.24,"And unless you sort of revivify, unless you resuscitate, that name's gonna get wiped away."],[1083.1,"And then you notice having your name recorded there, your name not being blotted out, then is sort of paralleled with. Christ confessing that person's name before the Father and before his angels"],[1099.06,"And this is imagery, this occurs in uh St."],[1104.1,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, and we talked about it there."],[1108.42,"This imagery of when someone dies, the opposite of being forgotten."],[1114.99,"Is that you're sort of welcomed into God's presence."],[1119.23,"And this is the imagery of God sort of enthroned with his angels, and the saints, and Christ is coming and sort of introducing you."],[1130.15,"Sort of vouching for you."],[1131.93,"That sponsorship. This is this person."],[1134.55,"And you're now welcomed into this group"],[1142.56,"So interestingly, that letter to Sardis is one of the most negative."],[1147.66,"But also, in terms of describing the hope of the people who remain faithful there is one of the most positive."],[1155.04,"In terms of the promises."],[1159.92,"Verse 7. To the angel of the church in Philadelphia."],[1163.94,"This is not Pennsylvania."],[1166.06,"Just for the record."],[1169.7,"These things says he who is holy, he who is true, he who has the key of David, he who opens and no one shuts and and shuts no one opens."],[1183.02,"So that's uh Isaiah 22, verse 22, for people keeping track."],[1190.62,"Um I'm always picking on our Protestant friends, especially our Calvinist friends."],[1199.42,"So I have to be equal opportunity and point out to every Roman Catholic apologist who has said that St."],[1206.06,"Peter has the keys of the house of David, which allow him to open and no one can close, and close and no one can open, that Christ says he has them here in the book of Revelation."],[1219.1,"St. Peter's dead at this point."],[1221.98,"He's been martyred."],[1222.94,"So sorry, Roman Catholics"],[1231.94,"Yeah. I guess."],[1232.9,"He maybe he had them for a minute, but he didn't pass them on to his successors, I guess, because Christ has them here."],[1241.86,"Yeah. Let him know."],[1243.46,"I haven't gotten to do a sorry Roman Catholics in a long time."],[1246.26,"So I had to get one in."],[1247.54,"Like I said, equal opportunity."],[1250.34,"Yeah. Um. So uh and that that in in Isaiah it's talking about the the steward, of the house of David."],[1264.8,"Wh whichich, I mean, I'll hand it to our Roman Catholic friend.s"],[1267.96,"That is a clever thing, being like, oh see, like Christ is the son of David, he's the king, and he's uh St."],[1275.48,"Peter's a steward."],[1276.45,"That w it falls apart here though."],[1278.69,"Um this is the same key language that Christ is referring to when he talks about giving the keys to St."],[1294.53,"Peter, and when he talks about giving the keys to all of the apostles, and when he talks about giving the keys in Matthew 18 to the whole church."],[1307.01,"Um because the church is Christ's body."],[1313.07,"The apostles and bishops of the church are the icon of Christ."],[1319.28,"But they don't receive powers from Christ that Christ divests himself of that they can then wield unilaterally."],[1330.13,"So the fact that this is used in this way by St."],[1335.17,"John here is sort of key to realizing that this power always remains with Christ."],[1347.11,"It's not handed on to anyone else."],[1350.71,"And when the church as a whole utilizes the forgiveness or binding of sins, which the binding of sins is really sort of excommunication."],[1364.8,"That's used to bring about repentance when it doesn't exist."],[1368.4,"And forgiveness that's extended when repentance does forget does exist."],[1372.8,"When the church does this, they're doing it as the body of Christ."],[1379.09,"The church acts as the body of Christ."],[1383.33,"And uh old heads can go back to uh where i where i covered Matthew 18 in these Bible studies maybe seven or eight years ago, and hear me talking about that uh in that context more um but it's important that it's here and um just to just to take an extra uh cheap shot at uh rudolf Boltman um this is a 20th century German um so there was there there was and probably still is somewhere in fact I think I've seen I'm not I won't mention his name anyway, uh there was a school of thought that that tried to divide early Christianity into sort of these apostolic schools."],[1439.41,"This has mostly fallen out of favor now."],[1441.65,"Th'eres sort of a light L I T E version of it now, where you talk about sort of Pauline theology and Johannine theology as being subtly different or coming from different perspectives."],[1453.33,"But it used to be full on, like there was this Johannine community that followed St."],[1458.13,"John, and there was this Petrine community that followed St."],[1460.93,"Peter, and there was this Pauline community that followed St."],[1463.65,"Paul. Like these were separate Christianities."],[1468.87,"That believed different things and had different practices."],[1472.15,"And that's, I mean, any number of things have destroyed that now, you know."],[1477.51,"Um, from anybody realistically being able to hold it."],[1481.51,"But th this was one of those things that was used when they would try to pit sort of Johannine, it's B.oltman did this especially Johannine Christianity against Petrine Christianity"],[1493.39,"And Johannine Christianity was never identified with any actual real world group conveniently."],[1499.31,"But Petrine Christianity was always somehow connected to Roman Catholicism in the first century."],[1505.63,"I that's another reason why it's fallen apart."],[1508.35,"It was sort of totally ahistorical."],[1510.67,"But and this is one of the verses that they used to do that."],[1513.39,"They say, ah, see, the Petrine Christianity believed he gave the keys to St."],[1518.27,"Peter, and the Johannine Christianity said that he kept them and did give them to St."],[1522.51,"Peter. So see, they're butting heads."],[1525.95,"That's kind of ridiculous overreading."],[1531.55,"And overreading. and overinterpretation is a real problem"],[1538.79,"It's not just a problem in the Bible."],[1541.27,"It's a problem in pretty much all early Christian texts."],[1545.16,"Because what people will do, and they do this with the Bible, they do this with those texts, is they'll take some statement somewhere, and regardless of the context of the statement, it doesn't matter if the statement is a piece of theological poetry, it doesn't matter if the statement is part of a historical narrative, it doesn't matter if it's in an epistle, it doesn't matter what the context is, they'll take that statement and treat it as if it is a philosophical definition."],[1575.38,"So they'll take, oh, see, here Jesus talks about his father and his father's angels."],[1576.26,"So St John didn't believe that Jesus was uh fully God."],[1587.98,"And you go, what?"],[1591.7,"He's like, well, he didn't say our angels."],[1596.1,"This is the kind of stuff you find in Bible commentaries."],[1599.14,"Like, legit. People ask me all the time, what Bible commentary should I read?"],[1602.34,"I'm like. Because this is this is what you get."],[1606.38,"Or they'll take an early church document."],[1609.15,"That that uh what was what I saw recently?"],[1614.67,"Oh, uh Shepherd of Hermes."],[1617.27,"Th'eres a reference to Hermes. seeing the angel of the Holy Spirit"],[1622.71,"And then throwing even Greek grammar aside, they say, Oh, he's saying the Holy Spirit is an angel, so he's like an Arian."],[1633.43,"It's like, no, no, he didn't."],[1635.69,"The Holy Spirit doesn't have a form, so if he's gonna see the Holy Spirit, what's he gonna see?"],[1639.93,"An angel representing the Holy Spirit."],[1642.06,"So people take statements in isolation and then try to derive theology from them."],[1647.9,"And so they'll take bits and pieces of."],[1651.2,"So, first of all, they're gonna say revelation is written by somebody different than St."],[1655.6,"John's Gospel and Epistles, just on principle for no good reason."],[1659.42,"And then they're there they'll say, go through Revelation. and pick out, let's find all the statements we can find about Jesus"],[1666.9,"And then treat them like they're definitional statements and say, okay, this is the Christology of the author of the book of Revelation."],[1673.06,"And then they'll do the same thing to the first seven of St."],[1677.62,"Paul's epistles, because those are the only ones they acknowledge that St."],[1680.66,"Paul wrote. And they'll say, oh, well, this is St."],[1682.9,"Paul's Christology. And they'll see, look, they're different."],[1688.0,"Do you see the problem with that?"],[1691.1,"So someone someone could take my book, The Religion of the Apostles, say, I'm going to go through here and find all the places where Father Stephen mentions the Holy Spirit."],[1700.22,"And then I'm going to do the same thing."],[1701.42,"I'm going to treat those as definitional."],[1702.94,"I'm going to construct this is Father Stephen's doctrine of the Holy Spirit."],[1708.07,"And they probably accuse me of all kinds of heresies."],[1711.93,"Because they've taken these partial statements in different contexts."],[1716.81,"And so that's not how any of this works."],[1720.91,"Things that people write reflect what they believe."],[1725.63,"But they aren't definitional of what they believe."],[1732.11,"So when we're comparing two texts of scripture by two different authors, or we're comparing two different church fathers and their writings."],[1743.95,"And those right, say they're writing on the same topic."],[1745.95,"Say they're talking about the same verse of the Bible."],[1750.31,"Our very quick reaction is, well, he says this and he says this."],[1754.81,"And they're both claiming this is what the verse means."],[1757.85,"How do we reconcile."],[1760.38,"But again, it reflects what they believe about it."],[1763.1,"So for example, you could say, if someone believed in the doctrine of the Trinity, the way it was set out by the Council of Nicaea, could they make this statement?"],[1775.6,"Could someone who believed in the doctrine of the Trinity have Christ talking about his father and his angels?"],[1782.64,"Well, yeah. So most of the things that are presented to us as contradictory by scholars within the scriptures, within the fathers, between the scriptures and the fathers, are not actually contradictory."],[1806.33,"They're different statements in different contexts from people who believed mostly the same thing."],[1812.09,"Now, occasionally you'll find a statement like you could read something written by Arius and say, could someone who believed in the doctrine of the Trinity say this?"],[1820.49,"And the answer is no."],[1822.41,"And you say, okay, well, this is clearly then different."],[1830.58,"Well, yeah. Yeah."],[1832.5,"But so the the but but the idea is you can't sort of construct, you can't work from here's what this person said in a few different contexts, to this is the totality of what that person believed."],[1846.06,"There's not a way to bridge that."],[1849.84,"There's not a way to bridge that."],[1851.76,"And so if we have a statement from St."],[1855.36,"Paul that seems to say one thing and one from St."],[1857.44,"John that seems to say another thing, it's helpful to say, well, let's imagine for the sake of argument that they believed the same thing."],[1864.64,"Could someone make both statements consistently?"],[1868.16,"And a lot of times the answer is yes."],[1869.92,"The same person could make both statements without contradicting themselves."],[1875.58,"Because it's two different contexts, it's two different. positions"],[1881.06,"So uh I think in this Bible study, this is the first time I've laid that."],[1886.5,"I I've made the comment, I know, several times that if you understand the fathers correctly, they never contradict themselves."],[1891.62,"But this is what I'm talking about."],[1894.36,"This this is this is what I'm talking about."],[1896.6,"So rather than trying to find contradictions and problems when we read the scriptures, and trying to extract beliefs based on statements in context."],[1911.33,"We need to look at the statements as proceeding from a person who has a whole bunch of beliefs."],[1915.11,"Like St. Paul believed a whole bunch of things that he never wrote about in his epistles that we have."],[1923.95,"Well, there's a lot of topics he didn't talk about."],[1926.49,"You know what I mean?"],[1927.37,"I mean, we don't know exactly what he believed on certain issues."],[1930.61,"Like, did did St."],[1931.57,"Paul think first Maccabees was authoritative?"],[1935.97,"We don't know."],[1937.33,"He probably believed it was or it wasn't."],[1939.58,"I would wager he knew it existed, so he had a belief on it."],[1943.66,"But he never tells us."],[1945.95,"Is some of this like a symptom of our time so it was like people take such extreme, like it seems like if you're saying that contradiction, it's like we seem to just like take one thing and then just because it's slightly different, we just have against each other as opposed to kind of we tend to try to try to pit things against each other rather than reconcile them."],[1963.63,"We break down instead. of synthesizing"],[1966.55,"And that's partially the it's our 18th and 19th century German friends."],[1971.69,"Of critical, you know, pull it apart."],[1975.31,"Look for the look for the contradictions."],[1977.71,"That's that's Hegel all over, is look for the contradictions."],[1981.15,"Um but uh do those things, but also re the idea of reconstruction."],[1986.59,"The idea of treating a text like the biblical text or an ancient patristic text or any ancient text, as a piece of historical evidence or archaeological, even evidence from which we try to reconstruct."],[2000.61,"The the author and the world."],[2003.55,"And in most cases, that's not possible."],[2006.51,"In most cases, what you get is a ton of guesswork"],[2012.13,"And when you read studies of the ancient Near East, it's amazing the level of guesswork."],[2018.45,"It's amazing. And you'll even have periods where there's complete scholarly agreement on something for which there's almost no evidence."],[2027.55,"It's just a reconstruction."],[2028.99,"And then at the end of that period, someone will come along and say, like, hey, you know what?"],[2032.43,"This is not built on anything."],[2034.43,"Here's a better explanation."],[2036.19,"And then everybody will sign on to that, even though there's he's using the same evidence as the other guys."],[2041.55,"It's just for whatever reason the new reconstruction is more appealing."],[2045.07,"Maybe it's more feminist or it's more uh you know, politically on one side or the other, you know."],[2050.11,"Like more conservative, more whatever"],[2055.51,"I got frustrated ultimately because I've I read these authoritative scholars who said that this is what happened, and I'm not reading today at one point."],[2067.51,"Yeah. Okay, this is what happened, I'll teach it."],[2070.23,"And then ten years later, that's not what happened at all."],[2074.55,"And so I've gradually went through from teaching ancient changes."],[2079.59,"Yeah. Yeah, and whatever the politics going on at the time uh and fads."],[2087.27,"Not laziness that we see."],[2091.21,"I mean, there's a there's only so much you can have to learn."],[2094.22,"And and take the authorities were when we're talking about that scientific approach, it's all based in the myth of objectivity."],[2104.18,"It's all based in the myth that I can I can achieve the view from nowhere."],[2110.95,"I can look at things from no particular perspective."],[2116.28,"Or from all the perspectives at once."],[2118.28,"And that's not possible for any human."],[2124.76,"Yeah. Yeah. Uh just a few hours, not so long"],[2133.48,"But that's that help."],[2136.68,"You know it is not a bit."],[2139.68,"I mean, you could even say God's seeing it from God's perspective because he's personal, but and we can't fathom what that would be."],[2146.48,"But we as humans are always looking at things from a perspective."],[2151.7,"Of our own time and place, our own beliefs, our own values."],[2156.04,"That's gonna affect what we look at, what we see when we look at anything, the past, the present."],[2163.68,"Everything. Even science, I hate to tell scientists."],[2167.7,"The the science is the one place where the myth of objectivity is still around."],[2172.1,"But what gets studied, how much it gets studied, what experiments get repeated and which ones don't, what gets investigated and what doesn't get investigated, how results are interpreted and applied, how policy is made from those results, those are all completely subjective."],[2199.02,"Yeah. Like that's not a personal one."],[2201.18,"Yeah. You never you're you won't go and kind of cast your your own culture into the culture that you're trying to study as a choice of it."],[2209.32,"And that that doesn't mean we're sort of doomed like to postmodern subjectivity."],[2213.64,"It's just you have to be aware."],[2216.46,"If you're pretending you're being objective, that means you're not aware of all your biases and presuppositions."],[2222.38,"But if you're aware of your things seems to be subjective, it will I was going to say a history professor would just drill into us."],[2239.3,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2242.82,"Yeah. And so you have to say I'm looking at this from my perspective now."],[2249.15,"And that's that's w how I am coming at it."],[2253.15,"And the more aware we become of those things, the less bound by them."],[2258.88,"That we are."],[2260.2,"Well you reminded me when you were talking about the contradictions of NCM seek and known, which is what the twelfth century."],[2267.96,"Yeah. And I haven't read, it but you think of Abelard?"],[2269.52,"Yes Abel's going to say."],[2269.6,"but And um as I understand from what I read about it, he's finding all the contradictions he can and putting them next to it."],[2282.04,"Yeah. Secret note is literally yes and no."],[2284.44,"Yeah. And that's quite a summary."],[2289.24,"It's quantum. Yeah."],[2291.8,"Yes, ancient that they mean why all that is to say um if we're we're going to uh take seriously the idea that we're dealing with the scriptures in canon. which is something we're free to do because we're Orthodox Christians"],[2321.14,"Most of us here."],[2322.42,"Don't want to leave anybody out."],[2326.1,"So if in the Protestant world, if you're gonna adhere to sola scriptura, there's other things."],[2332.23,"Um in terms of the earliest text, the most reliable text, what version of the text."],[2337.35,"But for us as Orthodox Christians, the canonical version of the text is the text."],[2341.79,"The text is received by the church is is the text."],[2345.47,"So, for example, the church received four gospels."],[2350.51,"They received four gospels at once."],[2353.47,"There is no period of time in the history of the church where there's one church using one of the four gospels and another one using a different one, and they all get together and it's like peanut butter and chocolate in a Reese's commercial and they just throw it all together."],[2366.0,"It's they have all four."],[2370.0,"And you've probably noticed there's differences in details between them."],[2377.09,"And the church received all four of them anyway, and especially early on, did not seem to have a problem with that."],[2387.25,"So with the Orthodox view of Scripture, we're dealing with Scripture in canon."],[2394.26,"So the four Gospels with differences in the details."],[2398.44,"Is how we've received scripture."],[2400.88,"But that means the mind of the church, the phronema of the church, received those four as they are."],[2408.74,"So the phronym of the church, that mind was able to make and affirm all of the statements in all four of them."],[2423.38,"So we can say that, yes, Saint Paul has one mind and St."],[2428.5,"John has another mind."],[2430.63,"And you could use that to put them against each other, but you also have the fact that the church included all of these in the New Testament."],[2438.31,"So there is a mind."],[2441.09,"Which we believe is essentially the Holy Spirit, who's able to affirm all of these things at once without contradiction."],[2451.85,"And so when we read this here, man, I took a long way around to get to this."],[2457.77,"We shouldn't be trying to contrast it with the other places that the keys are mentioned."],[2462.41,"We should approach it with how can someone affirm all of those statements in this one too?"],[2470.03,"And this one too."],[2471.15,"How does this all fit together into one holistic?"],[2473.87,"What is the idea underlying idea that could express itself in this way and in these different ways in these different contexts?"],[2484.75,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues. his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[2492.15,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2497.51,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":19.92,"lv":2,"h":"Revelation 3:1 - Sardis: a name, but dead","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:1</b> letter to <b>Sardis</b>: he who has the <b>seven spirits of God</b> and <b>seven stars</b></li><li class=\"d1\">identification picks up pieces of the <b>ch. 1</b> vision of risen Christ in glory</li><li class=\"d0\">More negative than the earlier letters</li><li class=\"d1\">last ones began \"here's the good things, but...\"; this one starts right off</li><li class=\"d0\">\"You have a name that you are alive, but you are <b>dead</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not every individual dead - the <b>organism of the community</b>; no life in it</li></ul>"},{"t":121.07,"lv":2,"h":"Revelation 3:2-3 - Wake up, repent, thief","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:2</b> \"be watchful\" = <b>wake up</b>; strengthen what remains, ready to die</li><li class=\"d1\">a few elements of community life not completely dead yet - \"almost dead\"</li><li class=\"d1\">take hold of those, <b>resuscitate</b> them → maybe revive the whole community</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:3</b> remember how you <b>received and heard</b> - hold fast and repent</li><li class=\"d1\">back when established; \"you do know better\" - go back to what you know</li><li class=\"d0\">Repent is <b>not a separate command</b></li><li class=\"d1\">repentance = not guilt, \"I'm sorry,\" getting off the hook</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>repair what's broken, heal what's wounded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= strengthen what's alive, <b>re-establish</b> how you were in the beginning</li></ul>"},{"t":267.11,"lv":3,"h":"Coming like a thief; \"visiting\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Thief imagery Christ uses in the Gospels (Matthew)</li><li class=\"d1\">owner who knew the hour would stop the thief; he comes when <b>nobody's watching</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Language of <b>visiting</b>: bishop visit = good</li><li class=\"d1\">this is \"<b>wait till your dad gets home</b>\" kind of visit - coming to sort things out</li><li class=\"d0\">If you don't begin to repent, watch, change → he shows up and you <b>won't see him coming</b></li></ul>"},{"t":349.85,"lv":2,"h":"Revelation 3:4 - A few who are worthy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:4</b> \"a few names even in Sardis\" not defiled garments → <b>walk with me in white</b>, worthy</li><li class=\"d0\">Distinction: church as <b>communal organism</b> vs. <b>collection of individuals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some going to the <b>Eucharistic gatherings</b> are not dead, followers of Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>guilt by association</b> when he sorts things out - about those not watchful</li></ul>"},{"t":402.67,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: what does a \"dead\" church mean?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: pictured a church where nothing's happening - this sounds like they're active</li><li class=\"d0\">A: we're <b>modern capitalists</b> - thriving = budget, giving, people in seats</li><li class=\"d1\">1st c.: no budgets; not about packing people in, \"73 baptisms this week\"</li><li class=\"d1\">hard to enter - <b>persecution</b>; not just anyone off the street</li><li class=\"d1\">origin of <b>sponsors</b>: a Christian had to <b>vouch</b> for you - \"not a robot spy\"; now more ceremonial</li></ul>"},{"t":519.48,"lv":2,"h":"Spiritual life and spiritual death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">About <b>spiritual life and death</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. John of Damascus</b>: physical death = soul separated from body</li><li class=\"d1\">spiritual death = <b>soul separated from God</b>, life cut off from the <b>source of life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We think physical = real death, spiritual = \"woo-woo\" analogy</li><li class=\"d1\">for St. John the <b>opposite</b>: real life = connected to God in Christ; biological life = <b>image</b></li><li class=\"d1\">physical death = image of and <b>result of</b> spiritual death</li><li class=\"d1\">Adam and Eve expelled: spiritually dead, cut off from <b>tree of life</b> → then physically die</li><li class=\"d0\">Church of Sardis: physically thriving, <b>spiritually dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like most of <b>Israel's history</b> - prospering politically, yet cut off from God</li><li class=\"d1\">cut off long enough → physical stuff <b>collapses</b> - what happens to Sardis unless they repent</li></ul>"},{"t":670.66,"lv":2,"h":"Revelation 3:5-6 - Overcomers and the name","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:5</b> overcomer <b>clothed in white</b>; name <b>not blotted out of the book of life</b>; confessed before Father and angels</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:6</b> \"He who has an ear...\" - end of every letter</li><li class=\"d0\">White garments for those living in the dead community who still follow Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Repeated reference to a <b>name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you have a name but you're dead\" = name in the <b>book of life</b>; church close to being blotted out</li><li class=\"d1\">3:4 \"a few names\" = people, still there and alive</li></ul>"},{"t":775.45,"lv":3,"h":"The book of life in the ancient world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Scattered through the Bible - first in <b>Torah</b>, last in <b>Revelation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Widespread <b>ancient Near Eastern</b> tradition</li><li class=\"d1\">Sumerians? unclear - untranslated tablets; \"translate grocery lists\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Babylonians, Egyptians, Assyrians, Syrians, Hittites; <b>Indo-European</b> (Greeks) version</li><li class=\"d0\">Usually <b>heavenly books or tablets</b>, kept by divine beings or angels</li><li class=\"d1\">Indo-European: the <b>fates</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everything a person does recorded → the divine <b>aware of everything</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Santa\" - today a heavenly database</li></ul>"},{"t":930.66,"lv":3,"h":"Memory and being blotted out","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Dead go to <b>Sheol</b> / <b>Hades</b> - exist as a shadow <b>as long as remembered</b></li><li class=\"d1\">memory fades → they fade</li><li class=\"d0\">Source of <b>\"Memory eternal\"</b>: if <b>God remembers</b> you, that's eternal</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>I know your works</b>\" at the start of each letter - he's got the book</li><li class=\"d0\">Name blotted out = <b>forgotten</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Psalm 88 (Matins): the slain \"whom thou rememberest no more\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Name known = <b>eternal life</b>: God knows you eternally</li><li class=\"d0\">Warning: \"still have a name but dead\" - unless you <b>resuscitate</b>, wiped away</li><li class=\"d0\">Name not blotted out parallels <b>Christ confessing the name</b> before Father and angels</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hebrews</b> imagery: opposite of forgotten = welcomed into God's presence</li><li class=\"d1\">God enthroned with angels and saints; Christ <b>introduces and vouches</b> - sponsorship</li><li class=\"d0\">Sardis: one of the most negative letters, yet one of the <b>most positive promises</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1159.92,"lv":2,"h":"Revelation 3:7 - Philadelphia and the key of David","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:7</b> to <b>Philadelphia</b> - \"not Pennsylvania, just for the record\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Holy, true, <b>key of David</b>: opens and no one shuts, shuts and no one opens = <b>Isaiah 22:22</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside to Roman Catholic apologists: Peter has the keys of David's house?</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ has them <b>here</b>, and Peter is already <b>martyred</b> - \"didn't pass them on\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"equal opportunity\" after picking on Calvinists</li><li class=\"d0\">Isaiah: <b>steward</b> of the house of David</li><li class=\"d1\">clever: Christ = son of David king, Peter = steward - \"falls apart here\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1278.69,"lv":3,"h":"Keys belong to the whole body of Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same key language as keys to <b>Peter</b>, to <b>all the apostles</b>, to the <b>whole church (Matthew 18)</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Church = <b>Christ's body</b>; apostles and bishops = <b>icon of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't receive powers Christ divests that they wield <b>unilaterally</b></li><li class=\"d0\">John's use here: power <b>always remains with Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Binding = <b>excommunication</b>, to bring about repentance; forgiveness when repentance exists</li><li class=\"d1\">church acts <b>as the body of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier Matthew 18 studies, 7-8 years ago</li></ul>"},{"t":1383.33,"lv":2,"h":"Excursus: Bultmann and apostolic \"schools\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Cheap shot at <b>Rudolf Bultmann</b>\" - 20th c. German school dividing early Christianity into <b>apostolic schools</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Johannine vs. <b>Petrine</b> vs. <b>Pauline</b> communities = separate Christianities</li><li class=\"d1\">mostly fallen out of favor; \"lite\" version: Pauline vs. Johannine theology</li><li class=\"d0\">Johannine never tied to a real group; Petrine tied to <b>Roman Catholicism</b> in 1st c. - ahistorical</li><li class=\"d0\">Used this verse: Petrine side gave keys to Peter, Johannine said he <b>kept them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"ridiculous <b>overreading</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1531.55,"lv":2,"h":"The problem of overinterpretation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Problem in the Bible and in <b>early Christian texts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Take a statement out of context - poetry, narrative, epistle - treat as a <b>philosophical definition</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"his Father and his angels\" → John didn't believe Jesus fully God: \"he didn't say <i>our</i> angels\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"what Bible commentary should I read?\" - \"this is what you get\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Shepherd of Hermas</b>: Hermas sees the angel of the Holy Spirit → \"Holy Spirit is an angel, so Arian\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no: Spirit has no form - sees <b>an angel representing</b> the Spirit</li></ul>"},{"t":1647.9,"lv":3,"h":"Statements reflect belief, not define it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Scholars: Revelation not by the author of the Gospel and Epistles \"for no good reason\"</li><li class=\"d1\">pick out statements on Jesus → \"Christology of Revelation\"; same with <b>first seven</b> of Paul's epistles → \"different\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Test: take his book <i>The Religion of the Apostles</i> - all mentions of the Holy Spirit as <b>definitional</b> → accused of heresies</li><li class=\"d0\">Writings <b>reflect</b> what they believe, <b>aren't definitional</b> of it</li><li class=\"d0\">Compare two authors or two fathers on the same verse: how reconcile?</li><li class=\"d1\">ask: could someone holding <b>Nicaea's Trinity</b> make this statement? - Christ speaking of \"his Father and his angels\": <b>yes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most \"contradictions\" = different contexts, same beliefs</li><li class=\"d1\">Arius: some statements <b>no</b> - clearly different</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't reconstruct the <b>totality</b> from a few statements in different contexts</li><li class=\"d1\">imagine same belief → same person could make <b>both</b> statements</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Fathers never contradict themselves\" if understood correctly</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul believed much he never wrote - e.g. did he think <b>1 Maccabees</b> authoritative? \"we don't know\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1945.95,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: pitting things against each other","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: is this a symptom of our time - we <b>pit things against each other</b> rather than reconcile?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: partly <b>18th-19th c. German</b> critical scholarship - pull apart, find contradictions - \"<b>Hegel</b> all over\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Plus <b>reconstruction</b>: treating text as evidence to reconstruct author and world</li><li class=\"d1\">usually not possible - \"a ton of <b>guesswork</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">scholarly consensus on little evidence, then replaced by a more appealing reconstruction - more feminist, more conservative</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: frustrated teaching what was authoritative, then \"ten years later that's not what happened\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2087.27,"lv":3,"h":"The myth of objectivity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Scientific approach based on <b>myth of objectivity</b> - the <b>view from nowhere</b></li><li class=\"d1\">impossible for any human; even God's view is personal</li><li class=\"d0\">We always see from <b>our own time, place, beliefs, values</b></li><li class=\"d1\">science too: what gets studied, repeated, interpreted, applied = <b>subjective</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not doomed to <b>postmodern subjectivity</b> - just be <b>aware</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pretending objective = unaware of biases and presuppositions</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm looking at this from my perspective\" - more aware → less bound</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: <b>Abelard</b>, <i>Sic et Non</i> = \"yes and no\" - contradictions placed side by side</li></ul>"},{"t":2291.8,"lv":2,"h":"Scripture in canon: the mind of the church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">For Orthodox: the <b>canonical text received by the church</b> is the text (Protestants: sola scriptura, earliest text)</li><li class=\"d0\">Church received <b>four Gospels at once</b></li><li class=\"d1\">never one church using one and another a different one, then mixed - \"peanut butter and chocolate\"</li><li class=\"d1\">differences in details - church received all four anyway, no problem</li><li class=\"d0\">Scripture in canon = <b>phronema</b> (mind) of the church affirming all of them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul one mind, John another - but the church included all → <b>Holy Spirit</b> affirms all at once without contradiction</li><li class=\"d0\">So on the <b>keys</b>: don't contrast with other mentions</li><li class=\"d1\">ask: what <b>underlying idea</b> expresses itself in these different ways, in different contexts?</li></ul>"}]});
